title: Schema constraints
tags: Schema
kind: reference
summary: Which constraints apply to a field, what a type and a cardinality demand, and why Nothing is always allowed.

For a metarecord `E` and a field name `F`, the ''applicable constraints'' are those
on `F` of every group targeting `"*"`, and of every group whose targets include
one of `E`'s `mf_schema` values. A metarecord with no `mf_schema` is untyped: only
the global constraints apply. An `mf_schema` value that no group names is a plain
label, with no constraint.

`E` satisfies a constraint on `F` when:

* ''type'' — every row of `F` whose value is not `Nothing` has the declared type. `Nothing` is always allowed: saying "this field does not apply" must stay possible whatever the declared type.
* ''cardinality'' — the number of rows of `F`, `Nothing` rows included, is within `[min, max]`. `Nothing` rows count because they are explicit statements: `min = 1` means "the field must be stated", even if the statement is "does not apply".

All applicable constraints apply together, so two that declare different types for
one field make every non-`Nothing` value invalid there. That is not detected when
the schema loads — it depends on which types a metarecord combines — and shows as
violations on write.

!! What a schema cannot touch

* A reserved field (`mfr_*`, `mf_*` — [[Reserved fields]]): the schema is refused otherwise. So a schema can never block the daemon's own writes (the watcher, reconcile), nor `mf_schema` itself.
* Sync's state (links, snapshots), which is outside the data model. Values ''brought'' by sync arrive through the ordinary write endpoints and are validated like yours — and sync requires both repositories' schemas to be identical.
